WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.770
Reports people act on: weekly updates, insights, and your capstone.

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.280
This is the video for module 4 of Reporting and Performance Tracking.

3
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:29.340
For example, Ruth manages sales for a drinks distributor in Enugu. Every Friday by 5 pm she sends her manager a WhatsApp update. First, the headline: sales of 412,000 naira against a target of 400,000. That is 103%.

4
00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.350
Then what went well: a new school customer ordered 20 cartons.

5
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.700
Then the problem, stated plainly: the van broke down, and two customers complained about lateness.

6
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:49.710
Next week's actions: chase the 4 unpaid invoices by Tuesday, and call the two unhappy customers.

7
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.910
And help needed: approval for a van repair, with the 85,000 naira quote attached.

8
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:02.750
In under 80 words, her manager knows the result, the risk, the plan and his decision.

9
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.720
A monthly report starts with a short summary: the overall result, the biggest win, the biggest concern and your main recommendation.

10
00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:25.040
Then a KPI table. Compare each KPI with the target and with last month. Below target but improving is a very different story from below target and falling.

11
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.660
Add the one or two charts that matter most.

12
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.920
Then explain what the numbers mean, and what should be done.

13
00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:38.140
Finish with risks, and an appendix for definitions and corrections.

14
00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:48.990
Many readers see only the summary and the KPI table, so make those clear. And send it on time, even with one figure marked to be confirmed.

15
00:01:50.240 --> 00:02:06.860
Numbers alone rarely change anything. Insights come from comparing. For example, Bisi splits her weekly sales of 70,000 naira by day. Saturday alone brings 28,000, which is 40%, more than Monday to Thursday combined.

16
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:13.670
So what? Her customers order for weekend gatherings, and her order by Thursday posts work.

17
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:24.160
Now what? Add a Friday evening delivery slot for 4 weeks, starting 3 April, and track whether Friday sales grow without reducing Saturday.

18
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:31.610
A strong recommendation has a specific action, an owner, a date, the KPI it should move, and any cost.

19
00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:42.250
A funder wants to see that their money achieved what was agreed. Report against their exact indicators, with evidence, and show spending against budget.

20
00:02:43.500 --> 00:03:00.550
Halima's budget is 1,500,000 naira. She has spent 1,200,000. That is 80% of the budget, at about 83% of the project time. Spending is slightly behind, because two sessions were cancelled.

21
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.360
The remaining 300,000 naira will fund the final and catch-up sessions.

22
00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:17.590
And a money rule: many grant agreements need written approval before you move money from one budget line to another. Ask your funder before spending an underspend on something new.

23
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:31.920
Your capstone tracks a real metric for 2 to 3 weeks. For example, Grace runs a hair salon in Port Harcourt and wants to fill quiet weekday slots. Her baseline is 12 weekday bookings a week.

24
00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:39.480
Her SMART target is 15 a week by the end of week 3. 12 times 1.25 is 15.

25
00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.300
Her action is a Tuesday and Wednesday discount for students.

26
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:53.120
She gets 13, then 15, then 17 bookings. An average of 15, which is 100% of target.

27
00:03:54.370 --> 00:04:08.000
Her insight: 9 of the 17 week 3 bookings were students, and 4 of them also bought a hair product. So she recommends keeping the discount another month, and tracking product sales to check it stays profitable.

28
00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:13.960
Disappointing results are fine. The capstone is judged on honest tracking and reporting.

29
00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:27.610
Before you submit, check every item. Goal, KPIs and target stated. Data checked. An honest chart. An insight that explains why. A recommendation with an owner and a date.

30
00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:35.590
And remove names and phone numbers. Share it as a PDF, or as a link set to Restricted, with the reviewer as Viewer.

31
00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:48.360
To remember. Keep weekly updates short and in five parts. Compare every KPI with the target and last month. Turn numbers into insights with What, So what, Now what.

32
00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:54.500
Give funders their agreed indicators, evidence and budget use. And share reports safely.

33
00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:12.260
Now practise. Write a weekly update in under 120 words, and set up your monthly report template. Write three What, So what, Now what rows from your own dashboard. Then start your capstone, with your baseline and SMART target at the top of your sheet.

34
00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:14.550
Continue with the lessons below.
